# Project Ownership
- The repository root is the PRIMARY and ACTIVE React application.
- The `oldcode/` directory is REFERENCE-ONLY.
- Never modify files inside `oldcode/`.

# Development Scope
- All new development, fixes, and refactors must be done in the root project.
- Treat `oldcode/` strictly as a source of reference for logic patterns.

# Migration Policy
- Allowed to manually migrate from `oldcode/`:
  - API service functions
  - Business logic
  - Authentication logic
  - Custom hooks
  - Utility/helper functions
- Migration must be selective and manual.

# Forbidden Copying
- Do NOT copy entire folders from `oldcode/`.
- Do NOT copy:
  - App.jsx / main.jsx / index.js
  - Routing configuration
  - Layout components
  - Theme or global CSS files
  - Build configs or scripts
  - Assets (icons, images, fonts)

# Routing & Layout
- Preserve the template’s routing and layout structure.
- Adapt old pages to the template layout instead of altering layout code.
- Follow the template navigation and sidebar patterns.

# Styling Rules
- Use only the styling system provided by the Material Dashboard Pro React template.
- Do not import CSS, SCSS, or theme files from `oldcode/`.
- Do not mix UI frameworks.

# Dependency Rules
- Install dependencies only when required by migrated logic.
- Never copy `package.json` entries wholesale from `oldcode/`.
- Prefer existing dependencies already used by the template.

# Environment Variables
- Follow the template’s environment variable format.
- Do not reuse old environment variable names without verification.

# Testing & Build
- Test functionality page-by-page after each migration.
- Ensure `npm run build` succeeds before committing changes.

# Deployment
- Build and deploy ONLY the root project.
- The `oldcode/` directory must never be included in deployment output.

# Safety Rules
- If unsure about copying code, rewrite or refactor instead.
- Keep migrations incremental and reversible.



# Webix Integration Rules
- Webix is used strictly for UI rendering and record display.
- All Webix-related files must live inside the `webix_code/` directory.
- Do NOT move Webix files into the React `src/` directory.

# Responsibility Separation
- React controls:
  - Routing
  - Data fetching
  - Authentication
  - State management
- Webix controls:
  - Data tables
  - Forms
  - Trees
  - Charts
  - Layouts for displaying records only

# Data Flow Rules
- Webix must NEVER directly call APIs.
- All API calls must be done in React services or shared logic.
- Data must be passed to Webix via:
  - `data` property
  - `parse()`
  - `clearAll()` + `parse()`
  - `load()` only when triggered by React

# Webix Lifecycle Rules
- Webix UI must be initialized only after React component mount.
- Webix UI must be destroyed on React component unmount.
- Prevent multiple Webix instances for the same container.

# Webix Code Restrictions
- No business logic inside Webix config files.
- No authentication logic inside Webix code.
- No environment variable usage inside Webix configs.
- No direct DOM manipulation outside Webix APIs.

# Styling Rules (Webix)
- Use the Webix theme styles provided with the purchased theme.
- Do NOT override Webix CSS globally from React.
- Do NOT mix Webix UI and Material UI components on the same screen.

# Migration Usage
- `oldcode/` may be referenced to understand:
  - API response structure
  - Data transformation logic
- UI from `oldcode/` must NOT be recreated inside Webix unless explicitly required.

# Performance & Data Volume
- Webix components must support large datasets efficiently.
- Prefer paging, lazy loading, or server-side filtering when available.
- Avoid rendering large datasets directly without optimization.

# Deployment Safety
- Ensure `webix_code/` is bundled only as UI assets.
- Verify Webix licenses are not exposed in client-side code.
- Confirm Webix UI works in production build mode.

# Safety Rules (Webix)
- If Webix requires logic, move that logic to React services.
- Keep Webix as a pure view layer.
- Any uncertainty defaults to React-side implementation.
